<h2 id="gradients">
Gradients</h2>

	A gradient is an image that smoothly fades from one color to another.
	These are commonly used for subtle shading in background images, buttons, and many other things.
	The <dfn export lt="gradient function">gradient functions</dfn> described in this section allow an author to specify such an image in a terse syntax,
	so that the UA can generate the image automatically when rendering the page.
	The syntax of a <<gradient>> is:

	<pre class=prod>
		<dfn>&lt;gradient></dfn> = [
			<<linear-gradient()>> | <<repeating-linear-gradient()>> |
			<<radial-gradient()>> | <<repeating-radial-gradient()>> |
			<<conic-gradient()>>  | <<repeating-conic-gradient()>> |
			<<mesh-gradient()>> | <<freeform-gradient()>> ]
	</pre>

	<div class='example' id='ex-gradients-are-images'>

		As with the other <<image>> types defined in this specification,
		gradients can be used in any property that accepts images.
		For example:

		<ul class=lang-css>
			<li><code>background: linear-gradient(white, gray);</code>
			<li><code>list-style-image: radial-gradient(circle, #006, #00a 90%, #0000af 100%, white 100%)</code>
		</ul>
	</div>

	A gradient is drawn into a box with the dimensions of the <a>concrete object size</a>,
	referred to as the <dfn export>gradient box</dfn>.
	However, the gradient itself has no [=natural dimensions=].

	<wpt>
		gradients-with-border.html
	</wpt>

	<div class='example' id='ex-default-gradient-box'>
		For example, if you use a gradient as a background,
		by default the gradient will draw into a <a>gradient box</a> the size of the element's padding box.
		If 'background-size' is explicitly set to a value such as ''100px 200px'',
		then the <a>gradient box</a> will be 100px wide and 200px tall.
		Similarly, for a gradient used as a 'list-style-image',
		the box would be a 1em square,
		which is the <a>default object size</a> for that property.
	</div>

	Gradients are either defined along one dimension (1D), like linear and conic gradients,
	or along two dimensions (2D), like mesh and freeform gradients.

	1D gradients are specified by defining the <dfn>starting point</dfn> and <dfn>ending point</dfn>
	of a <dfn export>gradient line</dfn>
	(which, depending on the type of gradient,
	may be technically a line, or a ray, or a spiral),
	and then specifying colors at points along this line.
	The colors are smoothly blended to fill in the rest of the line,
	and then each type of 1D gradient defines how to use the color of the <a>gradient line</a> to produce the actual gradient.

2D Gradients {#2d-gradients}
============================

	Issue: TODO add intro and move stuff about 2D gradients from the previous section here.
	Define the syntax for the 2D gradients here as an extension of the basic <<gradient>> type.

	While 1D gradients define [=color stops=] along a [=gradient line=],
	2D gradients are specified by defining a set of [=color points=] on a two dimensional [=gradient plane=],
	and then smoothly blending the colors
	to produce gradients between them.

	Each type of 2D gradient defines how to position the [=color points=]
	and how the gradient is produced.

Mesh Gradients: the ''mesh-gradient()'' notation {#mesh-gradients}
------------------------------------------------------------------------

	A mesh gradient is a 2D gradient
	created by specifying a two dimensional grid-like mesh,
	with a color defined at each vertex on the mesh.
	Each cell in the grid-like mesh is known as a <i>patch</i>
	and the colors at the four vertices (i.e. corners) of the patch are interpolated
	to produce a smooth gradient.

	A mesh can have any number of rows and columns.
	Each vertex can be positioned anywhere on the mesh
	and the edges can be curved with control points.

	Note: Be careful when using a grid metaphor to describe a mesh.
	Typically to specify the number of rows and columns a grid has,
	you declare the number of cells in a row or column.
	However, to specify the size of a mesh gradient,
	you describe the number of vertices in a row or column
	(i.e. the points where the mesh lines intersect).

	<div class='example' id='ex-mesh-gradient-visualization'>
		This example creates a simple mesh
		that has three mesh points per row and three rows in total:

		<pre class="lang-css">
			background-image: mesh-gradient(
				red,  blue, red;
				blue, red,  blue;
				red,  blue, red
			);
		</pre>

		The lines of the mesh are illustrated in the following image:

		<img alt="There are three horizontal and three vertical lines going through the mesh."
		     src="images/mesh-gradient-grid.png"
		     width="372" height="366">
	</div>

<h4 class='no-toc' id='mesh-gradient-syntax'>
''mesh-gradient()'' Syntax</h4>

	The ''mesh-gradient()'' function takes a list of <<mesh-point>>s
	made up of a color and optional positioning information.
	The mesh points are specified as a grid.
	Each mesh point on a row is separated by a comma.
	A semicolon starts a new row.

	All rows must have an equal number of mesh points on them.
	If there is an inconsistent number of mesh points specified in the rows,
	the function is invalid.

	There must be a minimum of two rows and two columns of mesh points
	to be able to produce a patch;
	otherwise the function is invalid.

	The syntax for a mesh gradient is:

	<pre class='prod'>
		<dfn>mesh-gradient()</dfn> =
			mesh-gradient( <<color-interpolation-method>>?, <<mesh-point>>#{2,} [ ';' <<mesh-point>>#{2,} ]+ )

		<dfn>&lt;mesh-point></dfn> = <<color>> [ <<mesh-absolute-position>> | <<mesh-relative-position>> ]?
		<dfn>&lt;mesh-absolute-position></dfn> = <<position>> [ with <<position>> [ / <<position>> ]{0,3} ]?
		<dfn>&lt;mesh-relative-position></dfn> = by <<coordinate-pair>> [ with <<mesh-relative-control-point>> [ / <<mesh-relative-control-point>> ]{0,3} ]?
		<dfn>&lt;mesh-relative-control-point></dfn> = <<coordinate-pair>> [from origin]?
	</pre>


	Issue: Should absolute positioned mesh points still be able to have relative control points?
	We could include relative control point coordinates in the absolute positioning, as per the shapes() function.

	Issue: Should we have a way to make the mesh point positions optional while still specifying control points?
	No, because you can just specify a relative position of 0 0, so make a note to explain that.

	The arguments are defined as follows:

	<dl dfn-type=value dfn-for="mesh-gradient()">
		<dt><dfn><<mesh-point>></dfn> = <<color>> [ <<mesh-absolute-position>> | <<mesh-relative-position>> ]?
		<dd>
			Adds a vertex on the grid with a specified <<color>>.
			The position is optional
			and can be specified using either <<mesh-absolute-position>> or <<mesh-relative-position>>.
			If no position is specified, the point is positioned at the [=default mesh point position=].

		<dt><dfn><<mesh-absolute-position>></dfn> = <<position>> [ with <<position>> [ / <<position>> ]{0,3} ]?
		<dd>
			Defines the position of a mesh point as an absolute position within the [=gradient box=].
			Any positions defined after the 'with' keyword represent control points
			for the mesh point's Bézier curve.

			Control points are defined in the standard "top, right, bottom, left" order
			(similar to properties like 'margin').
			If omitted, a control point takes its value from the specified point on the opposite side,
			rotated 180 degrees around the mesh point.
			(The second point, if omitted, takes its value from the first, rotated 90 degrees.)

		<dt><dfn><<mesh-relative-position>></dfn> = by <<coordinate-pair>> [ with <<mesh-relative-control-point>> [ / <<mesh-relative-control-point>> ]{0,3} ]?
		<dd>
			Defines the position of a mesh point relative to its [=default mesh point position=].
			Omitted control points behave the same as with absolute position control points, as explained above.

		<dt><dfn><<mesh-relative-control-point>></dfn> = <<coordinate-pair>> [from origin]?
		<dd>
			Provides a control point to a quadratic or cubic Bézier curve.
			When a <css>from</css> keyword is specified followed by <css>origin</css>,
			the given <<coordinate-pair>> is relative to the [=reference box=].
			If such component is not provided, the <<coordinate-pair>> is relative to the [=default mesh point position=].
	</dl>

	The <dfn export>default mesh point position</dfn>
	is the position of the mesh point if no explicit position is specified.
	To calculate the default mesh point positions,
	the [=gradient box=] is divided into an MxN grid,
	where M is the number of rows of mesh points
	and N is the number of columns of mesh points.
	Each row and column is spaced evenly,
	with the first and last rows and columns aligned with the edges of the [=gradient box=].

	<div class='example' id='ex-default-mesh-point-position'>
		On a 3x4 mesh grid, the default mesh point positions
		are equally distributed across the gradient box.
		The rows are positioned at 0%, 50%, and 100% of the height of the gradient box.
		The columns are positioned at 0%, 33.333...%, 66.666...%, and 100% of the width of the gradient box.

		Therefore, if a box is 300px wide and 200px tall,
		the default mesh point positions would have
		rows at 0px, 100px, and 200px
		and columns at 0px, 100px, 200px, and 300px

		TODO: Image
	</div>

	TODO

	Issue: We could let the author specify a background color for the mesh gradient,
	which would be painted in the areas of the gradient box outside of the mesh.
	Do authors have use case for that,
	or is it enough for authors to set a background-color?

	Note: Authors are free to define patches that overlap each other,
	but that can lead to unexpected results.

<h4 id='mesh-gradient-examples' class='no-toc'>
Mesh Gradient Examples</h4>

	All of the following ''mesh-gradient()'' examples
	are presumed to be applied to
	a box that is 300px wide and 200px tall,
	unless otherwise specified.

	Issue: These example images were created in Affinity.
	They should be changed to images from a browser implementation
	once that becomes available.

	<div class='example' id='ex-mesh-simple'>
		The simplest mesh gradient has just one patch.
		The mesh points are on each corner of the box.
		It can be specified with or without a position for each mesh point.

		<pre class=lang-css>
			background: mesh-gradient(
				#f00, #ff0;
				#0f0, #00f
			);
			background: mesh-gradient(
				#f00 0 0   , #ff0 100% 0;
				#0f0 0 100%, #00f 100% 100%
			);
		</pre>

		<img src="images/mesh-gradient-1.png" alt="" width="300" height="200">
	</div>

	<div class='example' id='ex-mesh-control-point'>
		This example shows a mesh gradient
		with control points specified on the middle mesh point.
		The first example uses absolute positions for the control points,
		and the second example uses relative positions.

		<pre class=lang-css>
			background: mesh-gradient(
				#f00, #ff0, #ff0;

				#f00,
				#f0f center center with top 40% center / top 30% left 30%,
				#00f,

				#0f0, #0f0, #00f,
			);
			background: mesh-gradient(
				#f00, #ff0, #ff0;

				#f00,
				#f0f by 0 0 with 0 -10% / -20% -20%,
				#00f,

				#0f0, #0f0, #00f,
			);
		</pre>

		<img src="images/mesh-gradient-2.png" alt="" width="300" height="200">

		The lines of the mesh are illustrated in the following image:

		<img src="images/mesh-gradient-2-lines.png" alt="" width="329" height="234">
	</div>

