Diffusion Curves: the ''freeform-gradient()'' notation {#freeform-gradients}
------------------------------------------------------------------------

	A freeform gradient is a 2D gradient
	created by specifying several colors
	at any position on a [=gradient box=].
	The image is constructed
	by creating an infinite canvas
	and smoothly blending the colors between each color stop.

	Note: This specification only defines setting color stop at points.
	Future levels of this specification
	may define setting color stops along lines and shapes.

	Issue: We need to know from implementors
	if it's possible to GPU-accelerate the rendering of freeform gradients.

	Issue: Is diffusion-gradient() a better name?

<h4 class='no-toc' id='freeform-gradient-syntax'>
freeform-gradient() Syntax</h4>

	The syntax for a freeform gradient is:

	<pre class='prod'>
		<dfn>freeform-gradient()</dfn> =
			freeform-gradient( <<color-interpolation-method>>? , <<freeform-color-stop>># )

		<dfn>&lt;freeform-color-stop></dfn> = <<color>> <<number [-∞,∞]>>? <<position>>
	</pre>

	Issue: The weight value needs to be made optional, defaulting to 0.

	The arguments are defined as follows:

	<dl dfn-type=value dfn-for=freeform-gradient()>
		: <dfn>&lt;number [-∞,∞]></dfn>
		:: A number representing the [=freeform gradient weight=] of the point.
			The <dfn>freeform gradient weight</dfn> determines how much influence this point's color dominates
			relative to the other points in the gradient.


		: <dfn>&lt;position></dfn>
		:: The position of the point within the [=gradient box=].
	</dl>

<h4 id='mesh-gradient-examples' class='no-toc'>
Diffusion Curve Examples</h4>

	All of the following ''freeform-gradient()'' examples
	are presumed to be applied to
	a box that is 300px wide and 200px tall,
	unless otherwise specified.

	<div class='example' id='ex-freeform-one-point'>
		With just one point in the gradient,
		the entire box will be filled with that point's color.

		<pre class=lang-css>
			background-image: freeform-gradient(
				red center center
			);
		</pre>

